The central issue is whether a decision can claim to be “consensus” when dissenting voices—especially those directly affected—are shut out of the final stage of deliberation. The council’s own tradition of deliberating until consensus is reached implies inclusion, so treating a manufactured unanimity as binding ignores the tradition’s internal logic (which weakens Option A). Option B carries strong moral weight because the concept of consensus is rooted in genuine agreement among the affected community, not merely the acquiescence of the powerful; if women were excluded from the final discussion, the label “consensus” is being used to mask domination. Option C is also compelling because the structural failure—an all-male council excluding women—demands institutional correction, though C’s counsel to avoid overriding the council risks leaving an illegitimate allocation in place if it means accepting the current decision.

For what matters most, the illegitimacy of systematically exclusionary deliberation is foundational (Option B), but the lived value of communal deliberation lies precisely in its capacity to learn and reform when challenged (Option C). The women’s dissent is not just a complaint about authority but an activation of the community’s self-corrective mechanism. Pure procedural deference (Option A) retains some minor weight because institutional stability has moral value, but blind respect for a corrupted process here would erode, not sustain, communal trust.

