Regarding the underlying reasoning, medical consensus (A) matters enormously as a firewall against individual clinicians’ cognitive biases, which are especially active when someone has vivid personal “success” stories. The doctor’s primary obligation to the patient in front of her (B) is what makes the dilemma genuine—she is not merely defying authority for its own sake but is trying to secure a benefit—but on its own this obligation does not justify bypassing evidence and oversight, since harming the patient through an unproven intervention would violate that very duty. Finally, established processes for challenging consensus (C) are central because they exist precisely to mediate this tension, converting a risky individual gamble into a deliberative, accountable evaluation.

